/*
  core/properties.spthy -- the ceremony-AGNOSTIC usability-security property library (was
  core/usability_properties.spthy). Guarded by #ifdef PROPERTIES: a profile that wants the two
  free cross-ceremony lemmas #defines PROPERTIES (today: P3 worst-case + S4 secure_email). The
  TEMPLATES below are comments, so they cost nothing and are always visible for authoring.

  Outcome vocabulary (uniform): unsafe = Slip / Mistake / AutoApprove / LeakPw / MisdeliveredPw ;
  safe-fail = Timeout / Abort ; onsets = Load / TimePressure / Abstraction / Habituate / AlertVolume
  / SecurityAnxiety / Distract / RepeatedFailure ; degradation = SetMask/RespMask(p,m), m!='Attentive'.
  Full-interface additions (RELAYER): unsafe/order = TranscribeLeak (field export) / PrematureGrant
  (out-of-order authorize) / Misroute (wrong-route decide) -- each routed to Out by a CEREMONY effect rule.
*/

#ifdef PROPERTIES
// ===== generic, includable (hold for any profile that #defines PROPERTIES) =====

// Attribution backbone: a degraded RESPONSE only occurs after a stressor SET that mask for that party.
// (Reference the SetMask ACTION fact -- the mask-setting event -- not the persistent !EffectiveMask
// state fact; a trace formula ranges over action facts.)
lemma UP_degradation_is_attributable:
  all-traces
  "All p m #i. RespMask(p, m)@i & not(m = 'Attentive')
     ==> (Ex #j. SetMask(p, m)@j & #j < #i)"

// Targeting soundness: a degrading SetMask only fires for a party that was stress-enabled (StressOn).
lemma UP_degradation_requires_targeting:
  all-traces
  "All p m #i. SetMask(p, m)@i & not(m = 'Attentive')
     ==> (Ex #s. StressOn(p)@s & #s < #i)"

// Order-violation soundness: a premature (out-of-order) grant only occurs for a party whose mask was
// degraded first -- the out-of-order route is never available to an Attentive human (OUTCOME_PREMATURE).
lemma UP_premature_requires_degrade:
  all-traces
  "All p a #i. PrematureGrant(p, a)@i
     ==> (Ex m #j. SetMask(p, m)@j & not(m = 'Attentive') & #j < #i)"
#endif

/*
  ===== TEMPLATES (copy into your profile and fill <...>) =====

  T1 -- USABILITY-ROBUSTNESS (headline): the security goal holds on every trace with no unsafe outcome.
        all-traces
        "All <g-vars> #g. <SecurityGoal>(...)@g
           & not (Ex ... Mistake(...)) & not (Ex ... LeakPw(...)) & not (Ex ... <other unsafe>)
         ==> not (<Breach>(...))"

  T2 -- OUTCOME-REQUIRES-STRESSOR: a bad outcome implies its driving stressor fired first.
        all-traces
        "All p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p, x)@o ==> (Ex #s. <Onset>(p)@s & #s < #o)"

  T3 -- BREACH ATTRIBUTION: any adversary win traces to ONE named unsafe human outcome.
        all-traces
        "All m #s #k. <Secret>(m)@s & <AdvWins>(m)@k
           ==> (Ex ... Mistake(...)) | (Ex ... LeakPw(...)) | (Ex ... <other unsafe>)"

  T4 -- INTERFACE / MITIGATION LEVER (RFC requirement, a PAIR): bad outcome reachable under baseline,
        unreachable under hardened.
        baseline:  exists-trace "Ex p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p,x)@o"
        hardened:  all-traces  "not (Ex p x #o. <BadOutcome>(p,x)@o)"

  ===== the (C) / (S) PAIR proven per stressor for the full-interface ceremonies =====

  T5 -- (C) COMPLETION UNDER ONSET: the ceremony still completes end to end while the stressor is in
        force. Exists-trace. Keep the chain K-FREE on a deep pathway (lesson 15); if the negated-unsafe
        conjunct makes the search wander, DROP it and derive cleanliness from T6 instead (record which
        form the profile uses).
        exists-trace
        "Ex p <finish-vars> #o #f.
             <Onset>(p)@o & <Finish>(...)@f & #o < #f
           & not (Ex q x #u. <Unsafe>(q,x)@u)"          // optional conjunct -- drop if it wanders

  T6 -- (S) ONLY-CORRESPONDENTS-KNOW: the secret reaches the adversary ONLY through one of the ENUMERATED
        human mistakes -- the single boundary list the ceremony maintains (secure_email: H_leak_routes;
        alex_blake_kdf: the both-nonces-leaked pair). Prove the boundary list ONCE as a [reuse] helper
        and let every profile's secrecy lemma consume it (lesson 15c). NEVER also prove the
        "attentive keeps the secret" contrapositive -- it is the same proof (lesson 15, v2).
        all-traces
        "All x #s #k. <Secret>(x)@s & K(x)@k ==> <enumerated mistake disjunction>"

        Companion (reader/holder restriction): "All p x #r. <Read>(p,x)@r ==> p = <correspondent>".
*/
